<subsection xml:id="subsec-multivariable-functions">
    <title>Functions of Several Variables</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            In mathematics and engineering, many quantities depend simultaneously on several inputs.
            The temperature at a point in a room is a function of the three position coordinates;
            the pressure of an ideal gas depends on both its volume and its temperature.
            This subsection introduces real functions of several variables, discusses how their domains are determined, and explores two ways to visualise them: the graph as a surface in space, and the contour plot via level curves.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <definition xml:id="def-multivariable-function">
        <title>Real Function of Several Variables</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>D_f \subset \R^n</m>. A map
                <me>f \colon D_f \to \R</me>
                is called a <term>real function of several variables</term>.
                The set <m>D_f</m> is the <term>domain of definition</term> of <m>f</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-multivariable-function-basic">
        <title>Two Basic Examples</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>f \colon \R^2 \to \R</m>, <m>f(x,y) = x \sin y</m>.
                        For instance, <m>f(1,1) = \sin 1</m> and
                        <m>f\!\left(1, \tfrac{\pi}{2}\right) = 1</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>g \colon D_g \to \R</m>, <m>(x_1, x_2, x_3) \mapsto \sqrt{36 - x_1^2 - x_2^2 - x_3^2}</m>.
                        The square root requires a non-negative argument, so
                        <me>
                            D_g = \left\{ (x_1, x_2, x_3) \in \R^3 \;\middle|\; x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 \le 36 \right\},
                        </me>
                        the closed ball of radius <m>6</m> centred at the origin.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-multivariable-function-engineering">
        <title>Functions of Several Variables in Engineering and Physics</title>
        <p>
            Multivariable functions appear throughout applied disciplines:
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Harmonic oscillation:</em>
                        <m>x(A, \omega, t, \varphi) = A \cos(\omega t + \varphi)</m>,
                        where <m>A</m> is the amplitude, <m>\omega</m> the angular frequency,
                        and <m>\varphi</m> the phase shift.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Mechanical power:</em>
                        <m>P(W,t) = W/t</m>, where <m>W</m> is work and <m>t</m> is time.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Ideal gas law:</em>
                        <m>p(V,T) = RT/V</m>, expressing pressure as a function of volume and temperature.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-visualization-multivariable">
        <title>Visualisation of Functions of Two Variables</title>
        <p>
            There are two standard ways to visualise a function <m>f \colon \R^2 \to \R</m>:
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>graph</term> is the surface
                        <me>G_f = \left\{ (x, y, z) \in \R^3 \mid z = f(x,y) \right\}.</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>contour plot</term> displays several <term>level curves</term>
                        <m>\{(x,y) \mid f(x,y) = c\}</m> for chosen constants <m>c</m>.
                        This is analogous to elevation contour lines on a topographic map.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            We illustrate both visualisation methods with two concrete examples.
            For the paraboloid <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</m>, the graph is a bowl-shaped surface and the level curves are circles.
            For the saddle function <m>g(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>, the graph bends upward in one direction and downward in the other, while the level curves are hyperbolas.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-visualisation-paraboloid-graph">
            <caption>
                Graph of the function <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</m>.
            </caption>
            <image width="72%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        width=8.8cm,
        height=6.8cm,
        view={125}{28},
        domain=-1.5:1.5,
        domain y=-1.5:1.5,
        samples=36,
        samples y=36,
        colormap/viridis,
        axis lines=box,
        xtick=\empty,
        ytick=\empty,
        ztick=\empty,
        enlargelimits=false
    ]
        \addplot3[surf, shader=interp] {x^2+y^2};
    \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-visualisation-paraboloid-contours">
            <caption>
                Contour plot of <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</m>; the level curves are circles centred at the origin.
            </caption>
            <image width="58%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
    \draw[->, gray!70] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, gray!70] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
    \foreach \r/\lab in {0.55/0.3,1.0/1,1.45/2.1,1.8/3.2} {
        \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) circle (\r);
        \node[blue!70!black] at ({0.72*\r},{0.72*\r}) {\small $\lab$};
    }
    \fill[blue!70!black] (0,0) circle (0.05);
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-visualisation-saddle-graph">
            <caption>
                Graph of the function <m>g(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>.
            </caption>
            <image width="72%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        width=8.8cm,
        height=6.8cm,
        view={125}{28},
        domain=-1.5:1.5,
        domain y=-1.5:1.5,
        samples=36,
        samples y=36,
        colormap/cool,
        axis lines=box,
        xtick=\empty,
        ytick=\empty,
        ztick=\empty,
        enlargelimits=false
    ]
        \addplot3[surf, shader=interp] {x^2-y^2};
    \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-visualisation-saddle-contours">
            <caption>
                Contour plot of <m>g(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>; positive level curves open along the <m>x</m>-direction and negative level curves along the <m>y</m>-direction.
            </caption>
            <image width="62%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
    \draw[->, gray!70] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, gray!70] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=1.0:1.9, samples=120] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=1.0:1.9, samples=120] plot (\x,{-sqrt(\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=-1.9:-1.0, samples=120] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=-1.9:-1.0, samples=120] plot (\x,{-sqrt(\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=1.55:1.9, samples=120] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x*\x-2.4)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=1.55:1.9, samples=120] plot (\x,{-sqrt(\x*\x-2.4)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=-1.9:-1.55, samples=120] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x*\x-2.4)});
    \draw[red!70!black, thick, domain=-1.9:-1.55, samples=120] plot (\x,{-sqrt(\x*\x-2.4)});
    \draw[green!50!black, thick, domain=-1.3:1.3, samples=120] plot ({sqrt(\x*\x+1)},\x);
    \draw[green!50!black, thick, domain=-1.3:1.3, samples=120] plot ({-sqrt(\x*\x+1)},\x);
    \draw[green!50!black, thick, domain=-0.7:0.7, samples=120] plot ({sqrt(\x*\x+2.4)},\x);
    \draw[green!50!black, thick, domain=-0.7:0.7, samples=120] plot ({-sqrt(\x*\x+2.4)},\x);
    \node[red!70!black] at (1.45,1.65) {\small $c&gt;0$};
    \node[green!50!black] at (-1.45,1.65) {\small $c&lt;0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="ex-semisphere-graph">
        <title>The Upper Unit Semi-sphere as a Graph</title>
        <p>
            We investigate the function <m>f \colon D_f \to \R</m> whose graph is the upper hemisphere of the unit sphere.
        </p>
        <p>
            The domain must consist of all base points <m>(x,y)</m> that lie under the hemisphere:
            <me>D_f = \{ (x,y) \in \R^2 \mid x^2 + y^2 \le 1 \}.</me>
            For a point <m>(x,y) \in D_f</m>, the height <m>z \ge 0</m> satisfies <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>, giving
            <me>f(x,y) = \sqrt{1 - x^2 - y^2}.</me>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-upper-semisphere">
            <caption>
                The upper unit semi-sphere can be written as the graph of <m>f(x,y)=\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> over the unit disk.
            </caption>
            <image width="72%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        width=8.5cm,
        height=6.2cm,
        view={130}{24},
        axis lines=box,
        xtick=\empty,
        ytick=\empty,
        ztick=\empty,
        enlargelimits=false
    ]
        \addplot3[
            surf,
            opacity=0.8,
            domain=0:360,
            y domain=0:90,
            samples=26,
            samples y=16,
            shader=interp,
            colormap/bluered
        ] ({cos(x)*cos(y)},{sin(x)*cos(y)},{sin(y)});
    \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </example>


    <remark xml:id="rem-surface-of-revolution">
        <title>Surface of Revolution</title>
        <p>
            Rotating the graph <m>z = g(r)</m> of a function
            <m>g \colon \R_{\ge 0} \to \R</m> around the <m>z</m>-axis
            produces a <term>surface of revolution</term>.
            In terms of Cartesian coordinates, this surface is the graph of
            <me>f(x,y) = g\!\left(\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\right).</me>
            The function value depends only on the radial distance to the <m>z</m>-axis,
            which explains the rotational symmetry.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="ex-surface-of-revolution-simple">
        <title>A First Surface of Revolution</title>
        <p>
            Consider the function
            <m>g \colon \R_{\ge 0} \to \R</m>, <m>g(r) = \dfrac{1}{1+r^2}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rotating the graph of <m>g</m> around the <m>z</m>-axis yields the surface
            <me>f(x,y) = \frac{1}{1+x^2+y^2}.</me>
            This example shows directly how a one-variable function of the radius becomes a function of two variables.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-surface-of-revolution-simple">
            <caption>
                Left: the profile <m>z=g(r)=1/(1+r^2)</m>. Right: the corresponding surface of revolution <m>f(x,y)=1/(1+x^2+y^2)</m>.
            </caption>
            <image width="95%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}
        \begin{axis}[
            width=6.1cm,
            height=5.0cm,
            xlabel={$r$},
            ylabel={$z$},
            xmin=0, xmax=3.2,
            ymin=0, ymax=1.1,
            axis lines=left,
            samples=150,
            ticks=none
        ]
            \addplot[blue, very thick, domain=0:3] {1/(1+x^2)};
        \end{axis}
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[xshift=7.5cm]
        \begin{axis}[
            width=6.4cm,
            height=5.2cm,
            view={125}{28},
            domain=-3:3,
            domain y=-3:3,
            samples=24,
            samples y=24,
            axis lines=box,
            xtick=\empty,
            ytick=\empty,
            ztick=\empty,
            enlargelimits=false,
            colormap/viridis
        ]
            \addplot3[surf, shader=interp] {1/(1+x^2+y^2)};
        \end{axis}
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-surface-of-revolution">
        <title>A Surface of Revolution as the Graph of a Two-Variable Function</title>
        <p>
            We investigate the function by first showing that its one-variable profile can be extended continuously at the origin and then by rotating this profile around the <m>z</m>-axis to obtain a surface of revolution.
        </p>
        <p>
            Consider <m>g \colon \R \setminus \{0\} \to \R</m>,
            <m>g(x) = \dfrac{\sin(x^2)}{x^2}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Extending <m>g</m> continuously to all of <m>\R</m>.</em>
                        Using the Taylor series <m>\sin(u) = u - u^3/6 + \cdots</m>
                        with <m>u = x^2</m>:
                        <me>
                            g(x) = \frac{\sin(x^2)}{x^2}
                                 = \frac{x^2 - x^6/6 + \cdots}{x^2}
                                 = 1 - \frac{x^4}{6} + \cdots
                                 \;\xrightarrow{x \to 0}\; 1.
                        </me>
                        Setting <m>g(0) = 1</m> extends <m>g</m> continuously to all of <m>\R</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Describing the surface of revolution.</em>
                        Rotating the graph of <m>g</m> around the <m>z</m>-axis replaces
                        the distance <m>|x|</m> from the axis by the radial distance
                        <m>r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>.
                        The resulting surface is the graph of
                        <me>
                            f(x,y) = \frac{\sin(x^2 + y^2)}{x^2 + y^2},
                            \qquad (x,y) \ne (0,0),
                        </me>
                        extended by <m>f(0,0) = 1</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-surface-of-revolution-sinc">
            <caption>
                Left: the profile <m>g(x)=\sin(x^2)/x^2</m> away from <m>x=0</m>. Right: the surface obtained by rotation, extended by <m>f(0,0)=1</m>.
            </caption>
            <image width="95%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}
        \begin{axis}[
            width=6.1cm,
            height=5.0cm,
            xlabel={$x$},
            ylabel={$z$},
            xmin=-3.2, xmax=3.2,
            ymin=-0.3, ymax=1.1,
            axis lines=left,
            samples=200,
            ticks=none
        ]
            \addplot[blue, very thick, domain=-3:-0.1] {sin(deg(x^2))/(x^2)};
            \addplot[blue, very thick, domain=0.1:3] {sin(deg(x^2))/(x^2)};
            \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(0,1)};
        \end{axis}
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[xshift=7.5cm]
        \begin{axis}[
            width=6.4cm,
            height=5.2cm,
            view={125}{28},
            domain=-3:3,
            domain y=-3:3,
            samples=30,
            samples y=30,
            axis lines=box,
            xtick=\empty,
            ytick=\empty,
            ztick=\empty,
            enlargelimits=false,
            colormap/cool
        ]
            \addplot3[surf, shader=interp] {((x^2+y^2) &gt; 0.001) * (sin(deg(x^2+y^2))/(x^2+y^2)) + ((x^2+y^2) &lt;= 0.001) * 1};
        \end{axis}
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </example>

</subsection>
